"Bombieri-Vinogradov for multiplicative functions, and beyond the $x^{1/2}$-barrier (https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.06865)\nciteturn8academia11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.6 years ago; Title: Bombieri-Vinogradov for multiplicative functions, and beyond the $x^{1/2}$-barrierAuthors: Andrew Granville, Xuancheng Shao\nTitle: Bombieri-Vinogradov for multiplicative functions, and beyond the $x^{1/2}$-barrier\nAuthors: Andrew Granville, Xuancheng Shao\nDate: Mon Mar 20 17:41:11 2017\n\nPart-and-parcel of the study of \"multiplicative number theory\" is the study of the distribution of multiplicative functions in arithmetic progressions. Although appropriate analogies to the Bombieri-Vingradov Theorem have been proved for particular examples of multiplicative functions, there has not previously been headway on a general theory; seemingly none of the different proofs of the Bombieri-Vingradov Theorem for primes adapt well to this situation. In this article we find out why such a result has been so elusive, and discover what can be proved along these lines and develop some limitations. For a fixed residue class $a$ we extend such averages out to moduli $\\leq x^{\\frac {20}{39}-\\delta}$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen does the Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem hold for a given multiplicative function? (https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.05710)\nciteturn8academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.3 years ago; Title: When does the Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem hold for a given multiplicative function?Authors: Andrew Granville, Xuancheng Shao\nTitle: When does the Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem hold for a given multiplicative function?\nAuthors: Andrew Granville, Xuancheng Shao\nDate: Sun Jun 18 19:51:40 2017\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be $1$-bounded multiplicative functions for which $f*g=1_{.=1}$. The Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem holds for both $f$ and $g$ if and only if the Siegel-Walfisz criterion holds for both $f$ and $g$, and the Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem holds for $f$ restricted to the primes.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBombieri-Vinogradov for multiplicative functions, and beyond the x1/2-barrier - UCL Discovery (https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/1556736/)\nciteturn8search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Granville, A; Shao, X; (2019) Bombieri-Vinogradov for multiplicative functions, and beyond the x1/2-barrier. ... Language:  | English\n\n## Bombieri-Vinogradov for multiplicative functions, and beyond the x1/2-barrier\n\nGranville, A; Shao, X; (2019) Bombieri-Vinogradov for multiplicative functions, and beyond the x1/2-barrier. Advances in Mathematics , 350 pp. 304-358. 10.1016/j.aim.2019.04.055. Image: An open access version is available from UCL Discovery\n\nImagePreview  | Text\nGranville 1703.06865v2.pdf - Accepted Version\nDownload (513kB) | Preview\n\n## Abstract\n\nPart-and-parcel of the study of “multiplicative number theory” is the study of the distribution of multiplicative functions in arithmetic progressions. Although appropriate analogies to the Bombieri-Vingradov Theorem have been proved for particular examples of multiplicative functions, there has not previously been headway on a general theory; seemingly none of the different proofs of the Bombieri-Vingradov Theorem for primes adapt well to this situation. In this article we find out why such a result has been so elusive, and discover what can be proved along these lines and develop some limitations. For a fixed residue class a we extend such averages out to moduli [Formula Presented].\n\nType:  | Article\nTitle:  | Bombieri-Vinogradov for multiplicative functions, and beyond the x1/2-barrier\nOpen access status:  | An open access version is available from UCL Discovery\nDOI:  | 10.1016/j.aim.2019.04.055\nPublisher version:  | https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2019.04.055\nLanguage:  | English\nAdditional information:  | This version is the author accepted manuscript. For inf