alogies to the Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem have been proved for particular examples of multiplicative functions, there has not previously been headway on a general theory; seemingly none of the different proofs of the Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem for primes adapt well to this situation. In this article we find out why such a result has been so elusive, and discover what can be proved along these lines and develop some limitations. For a fixed residue class a we extend such averages out to moduli ≤ x^{20/39−δ}.\n\n1. FORMULATING THE BOMBIERI-VINOGRADOV THEOREM FOR MULTIPLICATIVE FUNCTIONS\n\nThe Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem, a mainstay of analytic number theory, shows that the prime numbers up to x are “well-distributed” in all arithmetic progressions mod q, for almost all integers q ≤ x^{1/2−ε}. To be more precise, for any given sequence f(1), f(2), . . . , we define\n\nΔ(f, x; q, a) := ∑_{n≤x \\atop n≡a (mod q)} f(n) − 1/φ(q) ∑_{n≤x \\atop (n,q)=1} f(n).\n\nThe Bombieri-Vinogradov Theorem states that if f is the characteristic function for the primes, then for any given A > 0 there exists B =\n\nDate: April 22, 2019.  \n2010 Mathematics Subject Classification. 11N56.  \nKey words and phrases. Multiplicative functions, Bombieri-Vinogradov theorem, Siegel zeros.  \nA.G. has received funding from the European Research Council grant agreement n° 670239, and from NSERC Canada under the CRC program.  \nX.S. was supported by a Glasstone Research Fellowship.  \nWe would like to thank Dimitris Koukoulopoulos for a valuable discussion, as well as the referees for their careful readthroughs and comments.\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMultiplicative functions in large arithmetic progressions and applications (https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.04766)\nciteturn8academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.5 years ago; We establish new Bombieri-Vinogradov type estimates for a wide class of multiplicative arithmetic functions and derive several applications, including: a new proof of a recent estimate by Drappeau and Topacogullari for arithmetical correlations; a theorem of Erd{\\H o}s-Wintner type with support equal to the level set of an additive function at shifted argument; and a law of iterated logarithm for the distribution of prime factors of integers weighted by $\\tau(n-1)$ where $\\tau$ denotes the divisor function.\nTitle: Multiplicative functions in large arithmetic progressions and applications\nAuthors: Étienne Fouvry, Gérald Tenenbaum\nDate: Thu Apr  9 18:32:10 2020\n\nWe establish new Bombieri-Vinogradov type estimates for a wide class of multiplicative arithmetic functions and derive several applications, including: a new proof of a recent estimate by Drappeau and Topacogullari for arithmetical correlations; a theorem of Erd{\\H o}s-Wintner type with support equal to the level set of an additive function at shifted argument; and a law of iterated logarithm for the distribution of prime factors of integers weighted by $\\tau(n-1)$ where $\\tau$ denotes the divisor function.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRateBill7 (https://tenenb.perso.math.cnrs.fr/PPP/Billingsley.pdf)\nciteturn8search16 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 20.5 years ago; ** Let \\(\\{P_j(m): 1 \\le j \\le \\omega(m)\\}\\) denote the decreasing sequence of distinct prime factors of a positive integer \\(m\\). ... Billingsley proved in 1972 that the joint size distribution of the vector ... regarded as a random vector on \\(\\Omega_n := \\{m : 1 \\le m \\le n\\}\\) with uniform probability \\(\\nu_n\\), converges in law to the Poisson-Dirichlet distribution with parameter 1, which we shall denote by \\(PD(1)\\).\n\n# 1. Introduction and statement of results\n\nFor integer \\(m \\ge 1\\), let \\(\\omega(m)\\) denote the total number of prime factors of \\(m\\), counted without multiplicity, and write \\(P_j(m)\\) \\((1 \\le j \\le \\omega(m))\\) for the \\(j\\)th largest distin